      "text": "To determine the smallest number of gold coins that meet the given conditions, we need to find a number \\( n \\) that satisfies both of the following congruences:\n\n1. \\( n \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\)\n2. \\( n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe start by expressing \\( n \\) in terms of the first congruence. Since \\( n \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\), we can write:\n\n\\[ n = 6k + 4 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We substitute this expression for \\( n \\) into the second congruence:\n\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nNext, we simplify the congruence by reducing \\( 6k \\) modulo 5. Since \\( 6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5} \\), we have:\n\n\\[ 1 \\cdot k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nTo isolate \\( k \\), we subtract 4 from both sides of the congruence:\n\n\\[ k \\equiv 3 - 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written as:\n\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Substituting \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( n \\), we get:\n\n\\[ n = 6(5m + 4) + 4 \\]\n\\[ n = 30m + 24 + 4 \\]\n\\[ n = 30m + 28 \\]\n\nSo, \\( n \\) is of the form \\( 30m + 28 \\). The smallest positive \\( n \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\):\n\n\\[ n = 30 \\cdot 0 + 28 = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find out how many coins are left when these 28 coins are equally divided among seven people. We perform the division:\n\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\]\n\nSince the division is exact, there are no coins left over. Therefore, the number of coins left when equally divided among seven people is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
