      "text": "To solve the problem, we start by defining the number of coins in the box as \\( N \\). According to the problem, when the coins are equally divided among six people, four coins are left over. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\nThis means that \\( N \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we know that when the coins are equally divided among five people, three coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( N \\) can also be written in the form:\n\\[ N = 5m + 3 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\).\n\nWe now have two congruences:\n1. \\( N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\)\n2. \\( N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\)\n\nWe substitute the expression for \\( N \\) from the first congruence into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying the left side modulo 5, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSubtracting 4 from both sides, we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to \\(4\\) modulo 5, we can write:\n\\[ k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThis means that \\( k \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ k = 5n + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( n \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( N \\), we get:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 = 6(5n + 4) + 4 = 30n + 24 + 4 = 30n + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ N = 30n + 28 \\]\nfor some integer \\( n \\). The smallest positive value of \\( N \\) occurs when \\( n = 0 \\), which gives:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to determine how many coins are left when the coins are equally divided among seven people. We calculate:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\text{ R } 0 \\]\nThis means that when 28 coins are divided equally among seven people, there are no coins left over. Therefore, the number of coins left when divided among seven people is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
